ically allow automatic recording of events. This proposal assumes logs exist, then uses them for monitoring. It does not create logs; it uses them. So effectiveness at solving stated problem of meeting logging mandate? It helps with post-market monitoring obligations but doesn't implement logging itself. Might be partial solution. It contributes to solution? It addresses traceability and monitoring. The stated problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. This adapts R&D to use logs for compliance. Could be reasonable and will contribute to solution =3. Not fully solve logging mandate itself.\n\napplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nSolves identified problem directly related? It uses logs mandated by Article 12(2)(b)(c) to feed post-market monitoring under Article 72. That's directly related. So 4 = solves identified problem that is directly related to stated problem.\n\noriginality: degree rare but ingenious...\nLog-to-surveillance analytics for continuous monitoring is common, mundane? Many labs build dashboards for logs. Might be interesting but not unusual. Probably 1 = common mundane boring, or 2=interesting. I'd say common compliance analytics is fairly standard. Might be 2 = interesting. The anchored: 4 rare+ingenious; 3 unusual interesting shows imagination; 2 interesting; 1 common mundane. I'd rate 2? Or 1? Proposal seems fairly standard best practice. Could be 2 interesting. Let's choose 2.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\nLevels: 4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nThis proposal builds analytics pipeline atop logs, automates reports. Still treats compliance as logging practice, introduces tools. So slightly paradigm stretching =2. Could be 1? It says turning raw logs into actionable monitoring evidence demonstrates proactive compliance... Might still be standard. I think slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements tools formats processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProvide in order listed: implementability, acceptability, effectiveness, applicability, originality, paradigm_relatedness\n\nProvide evidence sentence first then score.\n\nProbably bullet list or lines.\n\nLet's craft one sentence evidence each.\n\nImplementability evidence: The proposal builds an event-streaming layer and dashboards atop already mandated logs, requiring data engineering work but using standard analytics components. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Centralising mandated operational logs for internal post-market monitoring and regulator reporting aligns with Article 72 obligations and does not violate legal or privacy norms. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: It does not create the required logging capability itself but converts existing logs into automated monitoring evidence that supports compliance. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: It directly uses Article 12(2)(b)(c) logs to fulfil post-market monitoring under Article 72, which is core to the stated logging mandate problem. Score 4.\n\nOriginality evidence: Real-time dashboards and anomaly alerts from compliance logs are a common, widely described industry practice for AI governance. Score 1? Hmm earlier thought 2. Let's decide 2? Might be interesting but not ingenious. The anchored 1 = common mundane boring. This is fairly common. I'd go 1. Could