        "content": "{\n  \"ratings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1184\",\n      \"acceptability_evidence\": \"The proposal implements exactly the statutory minimum logging required by Article 12(3) for Annex III 1(a) biometric systems, violating no legal or social norms.\",\n      \"acceptability\": 4,\n      \"implementability_evidence\": \"Deploying an immutable event log and a compliance dashboard for each biometric pipeline requires some engineering changes but is a reasonably feasible measures for an R&D lab.\",\n      \"implementability\": 3,\n      \"effectiveness_evidence\": \"Recording session times, reference database, matched input data and verifier identity directly fulfills Article 12(1)-(3) and would solve the logging mandate without regard for cost.\",\n      \"effectiveness\": 4,\n      \"applicability_evidence\": \"The four tracked data points are the identified statutory minimum for biometric identification systems under Article 12(3), which is directly part of the stated problem.\",\n      \"applicability\": 4,\n      \"paradigm_relatedness_evidence\": \"It introduces new tools, an immutable log and dashboard flags, but still treats compliance as logging practice rather than changing the lab’s R&D-compliance relationship.\",\n      \"paradigm_relatedness\": 2,\n      \"originality_evidence\": \"Audit trails for session times, database version, matches and human reviewer identity for mandated biometric logging is common, mundane compliance practice.\",\n      \"originality\": 1\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly...\n\nWe need rating for proposal P1184.\n\nProposal: Biometric Verification and Reference-Database Audit Trail. Purpose-built tracking layer for Annex III point 1(a) systems that records session start/end times, reference database consulted, matched input data, identity of natural persons who verifies results under Article 14(5). Operations team deploys immutable event log tied to each biometric pipeline; compliance dashboard flags any verification step lacking named reviewer or missing database version tag within 24 hours. These four data points are statutory minimum...\n\nRate subscales:\n\nacceptability: degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nThis is logging biometric data, immutable event log, tracking identity of verifiers. Acceptable? Logging mandated by law. Probably common strategies that violate no norms. Might be privacy concern but it's required. Probably 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Evidence: proposal implements exactly the statutory minimum logging required by Article 12(3) for biometric systems.\n\nImplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nPurpose-built tracking layer, immutable event log tied to each biometric pipeline; compliance dashboard flags within 24h. This is some changes, reasonably feasible? Might be significant change for R&D lab? Possibly 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could argue 2 significant change? Immutable log and dashboard might be moderate. Probably 3.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\nProposal directly records the four data points required by Art12(3). It would solve logging mandate. So 4.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem\nYes directly. 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nThis is purpose-built tracking layer for compliance. Introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? Probably 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Evidence says immutable event log and dashboard flags gaps. That's new tools but compliance remains logging practice.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nThis is essentially standard audit trail for biometric systems. Common, mundane. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or 2 interesting? The immutable event log tied to pipeline and dashboard flag within 24h might be somewhat interesting but overall it's statutory minimum. Likely 1.\n\nLet's pick scores:\nacceptability: 4\nimplementability: 3\neffectiveness: 4\napplicability: 4\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\noriginality: 1\n\nNeed one sentence evidence for each subscale before number.\n\nReturn rating echoing id exactly. For each subscale, give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProvide JSON? Probably output with fields.\n\nMake sure order listed: acceptability, implementability, effectiveness, applicability, paradigm_relatedness, originality.\n\nProvide evidence sentence then score.\n\nLet's produce concise output.\n\n",
