        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly...\n\nWe need rating for proposal P1237.\n\nProposal: Operational Logs as Training Signal. Treating structured operational logs mandated by Article 12(2) as first-class data asset for iterative model improvement, drift detection, post-market evaluation rather than discarding them. Two senior researchers + MSc student spend six months building feedback loops where logged edge-cases (Article 79 risk events) are automatically tagged, re-versioned into test suites, fed back into next training cycle. Converts pure regulatory cost into scientific capital, reuse existing compute and data pipelines for model refinement without additional external funding.\n\nWe need six subscales in order: originality, paradigm_relatedness, effectiveness, applicability, acceptability, implementability\n\nProvide evidence sentence then score.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious/imaginative/surprising; may be humorous. 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. 1 = common mundane boring.\n\nProposal: using logs as training signal. Is this unusual? In ML, using operational data for improvement is common. Using mandated logs specifically as first-class asset might be somewhat unusual but not surprising? Might be interesting. Could be 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Or 2 = interesting. Let's think reference class: experienced evaluator expects AI R&D labs to give responses to mandate. Many would see logs as compliance cost, discard after retention. Treating as data asset is maybe unusual but not rare? Might be common sense now. Could be originality 3? The anchored levels: 4 requires not expressed before AND ingenious. This seems not first time expressed. Probably 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchore