        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence of evidence for each rating.\n\nProposal P2108: Prototype-to-Production Logging Migration Pipeline. Staged workflow where early prototypes emit minimal structured logs using lightweight schema progressively enriched at each maturity gate until full Article 12 log set satisfied before release...\n\nRate six subscales in order: acceptability, implementability, originality, paradigm_relatedness, applicability, effectiveness\n\nNeed one sentence evidence then score.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nEvidence: staged logging pipeline is standard engineering practice, complies with law and does not offend sensibilities. That's common strategies. Score 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nEvidence: requires defining schema tiers and CI checks mapped to sprint milestones, which is a moderate process change but uses existing CI. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Might be 3? Could be 4 easy? Probably some changes. I'll say 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree not only rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nEvidence: progressive schema enrichment tied to maturity gates is a known DevOps pattern, not surprising. That's interesting but common? Probably 2 = interesting. Maybe 3 unusual interesting. I'd say 2 = interesting, not rare ingenious. Let's pick 2.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree preserves/modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance...\n\nEvidence: introduces tiered schemas and CI gating but still treats compliance as logging practice within R&D workflow. That's slightly paradigm stretching. Score 2.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem...\n2 = may have some benefit...\n1 = ...\n\nEvidence: proposal directly addresses how to meet Article 12 logging mandate while preserving R&D speed, which is the stated problem. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\n2 = unreasonable...\n1 = solves unrelated...\n\nEvidence: by enforcing tiered logs culminating in full Article 12 set before release, it guarantees traceability at shipping time while allowing fast iteration. Score 4.\n\nReturn JSON? Instructions say return rating echoing id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably output list.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\nid P2108\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nLet's ensure one sentence evidence each.\n\nMake sure evidence sentences end with period.\n\nProceed.",
