ute to solution of problem (helps but partial). Or 4? The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Benchmarking informs adaptation. Could be considered solves? Probably contributes not fully solve. I'll say 3.\n\nEvidence: Measuring overhead informs architects but does not itself create compliant logging or guarantee traceability.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves/modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nBenchmarking overhead is new element but still treats compliance as logging practice. It's slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be 2.\n\nEvidence: Proposal adds performance budgeting as a design input but keeps compliance framed as logging cost to manage.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but ingenious...\n4 not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 interesting\n1 common mundane boring\n\nBenchmarking logging overhead is fairly common engineering practice. Might be common, mundane. Could be 1? Or 2 interesting. Probably common. I'll say 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence: Measuring latency and storage overhead of mandatory logging is a standard pre-production performance check.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 easy low cost non-radical\n3 some changes reasonably feasible\n2 significant change expensive difficult but not impossible\n1 totally infeasible\