frastructure, ML expertise. For an AI R&D lab, reasonably feasible. Some changes. Might be 3? Could be 2 = significant change expensive. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. I'd say 3.\n\nEvidence: Building and calibrating anomaly detectors requires new models, data pipelines, and integration with logging, which is feasible for a research lab but not trivial low-cost.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard workability\n3 reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 unreasonable unlikely\n1 solves unrelated\n\nProblem: decide how to respond: meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources.\n\nProposal addresses Article 12(2)(a) identifying situations that may result in risk... It flags loggable events likely to indicate emerging safety risks. Does it solve logging mandate? It helps identify what to log, produce logs? The mandate is to technically allow automatic recording of events logs over lifetime. This proposal focuses on detection not necessarily logging infrastructure. It contributes to traceability but doesn't guarantee logging capability.\n\nEffectiveness maybe 3 = will contribute to solution, partial. It addresses identification of relevant events but doesn't implement mandatory logging itself. Might be 3.\n\nEvidence: The detectors help identify risk-relevant events to log but do not by themselves ensure automatic recording of all required logs mandated by Article 12.\n\nApplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 solves identified problem directly related\n3 solves implied problem related\n2 may have some benefit within special situation\n1 intervention not stated\n\nProposal directly addresses Article 12(2)(a) logging requirement. So applicability 4? It solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (meeting logging mandate). Might be 4.\n\nEvidence: