adigm preserving: standard compliance\n\nShared platform is basically centralising compliance plumbing. Introduces new tool but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? Might be 2. Not changing relationship between R&D and compliance fundamentally, just efficiency. So paradigm_relatedness =2.\n\nEvidence: It introduces a shared tooling layer but keeps compliance as separate logging practice rather than redefining how research is conducted.\n\nOriginality: degree rare and ingenious. Shared internal platform for logs is common, mundane. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring. Maybe 2 = interesting? Centralisation is common. I'd say 1.\n\nEvidence: Internal log aggregation platforms are a common, standard engineering practice in regulated R&D labs.\n\nAcceptability: socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies violate no norms. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Centralising logs for audit-readiness is legally aligned with Article 12 and does not offend sensibilities.\n\nImplementability: degree can be easily implemented. Infrastructure group two engineers allocate ~40% sprint capacity; project teams submit schema via API. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures? Might be 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible. Not easy low cost? Two engineers 40% is moderate. Could be 3.\n\nEvidence: It requires building a platform and ongoing engineering commitment but uses existing team capacity and simple API integration.\n\nReturn rating echo id P2153.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably as lines.\n\nProvide JSON? Probably just output.\n\nI'll output:\n\nid: P2153\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 4\n...\n\nLet's produce clean.\n\n",
