ndate and/or adapt R&D. A reference prototype does not actually meet mandate for production systems, just proves viability. Might be partial. I'd say effectiveness 3.\n\nAcceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Building a compliance prototype is common. So 4.\n\nImplementability: easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. One pod of 2 dev +1 researcher over eight weeks on existing test set. That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Maybe 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Eight weeks of 3 people is moderate. Could be 3. Might be 4 = easy to implement at low cost? Probably not low cost but reasonable. I'd say 3.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious... Common, mundane, boring =1? Building a reference prototype for compliance is common. Might be interesting but not unusual. Originality 1 or 2? \"interesting\" =2. I think building golden reference prototype is somewhat standard practice. Might be common mundane. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm. 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged. 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Prototype introduces reference prototype process but still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements tools/processes but still compliance as logging practice. Could be 2.\n\nAlternative interpretation: It is standard compliance practice. Probably paradigm preserving =1. The description says \"serving as lab's golden reference for any future product line\" - that is still standard.\n\nGiven instrument, likely 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged.\n\nLet's pick paradigm_relatedness 1? Or 2? Hmm \"introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\". A reference prototype is a new element. Might be slightly paradigm stretching.\n\nThe proposal de-risks every subsequent project by proving technical viability before commit budgets - that's a process change but not huge.\n\nI'll choose 2 for slightly paradigm stretching.\n\nLet's produce output with evidence sentences then scores.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer).\n\nProbably list in order.\n\nReturn rating echoing id exactly.\n\nLet's output:\n\nid: P2230\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences concise.\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal creates a production-grade logging prototype for Annex III 1(a) biometric verification, directly matching Article 12(3) logging requirements the lab must meet.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: It proves technical viability of the strictest logging fields before full product budgets, contributing to compliance but not itself deploying logs across all high-risk systems.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Building an internal compliance prototype and checklist is a routine engineering practice that violates no legal, social, or business norms.\n\nImplementability evidence: The work uses an existing test set and a small three-person pod for eight weeks to deliver a containerized prototype and checklist, which is reasonably feasible.\n\nOriginality evidence: Creating a golden reference prototype and compliance mapping for regulatory logging is a common, mundane de-risking practice in regulated R&D labs.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal adds a reference prototype and checklist tool but still treats compliance as conventional logging practice without changing the lab's core R&D-compliance relationship.\n\nScores as above.\n\nCheck scores: applicability 4, effectiveness 3, acceptability 4, implementability 3, originality 1, paradigm_relatedness 2? Might be 1. Let's reconsider paradigm_relatedness anchor: 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged. The proposal does introduce golden reference process, which may slightly change way of working. Could argue unchanged. Hmm.\n\nGiven typical rating, they'd likely see this as standard compliance. I'd lean to 1.\n\nLet's pick 1 for safety.\n\nFinal output.\n\n",
