ability 3? Or 4? Let's think: identified problem that is directly related = meeting logging mandate. The proposal does X (add budget line) to get Y (funding for compliance). Y is part of stated problem? Stated problem is how to meet logging mandate. Funding is enabler. Might be implied.\n\nI think applicability = 3? Could be 4: solves identified problem directly related - budget crisis when compliance surfaces late. That's related.\n\nI'll choose 3? Let's see effectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem. 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. Will adding budget line solve logging mandate? No, it just funds it. It contributes partially. So effectiveness = 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Yes.\n\nNovelty originality: degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious... Common, mundane? Adding compliance cost line to budget template is common practice in regulated industries. Probably common, mundane, boring => 1. Could be 2 interesting? I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm. Anchored: 4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nAdding budget line introduces new process element but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's slightly paradigm stretching? It changes funding process. Might be 2. Or 1 paradigm preserving? Standard compliance practice is budgeting for compliance. Hmm.\n\nThe description: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice = 2. So I'd pick 2.\n\nImplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented. 4 easy low cost or non-radical changes. Revising project request form and working with grants office is reasonably feasible. Might be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4? Requires organizational change but low cost. I'd say 3.\n\nAcceptability: degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies violate no norms = 4. Adding budget line is acceptable. So 4.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence for each before number.\n\nOutput format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably each on new line.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nP1906\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 3\netc.\n\nEnsure order: applicability, effectiveness, originality, paradigm_relatedness, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide one sentence evidence.\n\nMake sure evidence is one sentence.\n\nProceed.",
