s larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice\n\nStress test is standard compliance practice. Introduces audit process but still treats compliance as logging practice. Maybe slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements (stress tests). But still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2.\n\nOriginality: degree rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nQuarterly stress test for logging compliance is common audit practice. Probably common, mundane. So 1.\n\nProvide output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give _evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed format? Probably JSON? Instruction says return rating... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Likely lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1300\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nacceptability_evidence: Internal simulation audits are a standard, legally encouraged compliance practice that does not offend norms or sensibilities. acceptability:4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Setting up scenario scripts and quarterly reviews requires staffing and tooling changes but is reasonably feasible for an R&D lab with existing QA processes. implementability:3\n\neffectiveness_evidence: The stress tests identify logging gaps before regulators do, which contributes to meeting Article 12 but does not itself build the required logging capabilities. effectiveness:3\n\napplicability_evidence: Verifying start/end timestamps, reference databases, matched inputs and verifier identities directly addresses the Article 12(2)-(3) tra