tic capture of timestamps, reference databases, input matches and verifier identities directly fulfills Article 12(1)-(3) traceability requirements. Score 4.\n\nApplicability: degree clearly applies to stated problem. 4 = solves identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Yes. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: The proposal addresses how the lab meets the Article 12 logging mandate for high-risk AI systems. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree preserves or modifies paradigm. Anchors:\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nThis is building logging into architecture from inception, codify retention, CI gate. That's introducing tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? It says building compliance into architecture from inception avoids retrofits and signals traceable by design. Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nEvidence: It adds a reusable logging SDK and CI enforcement but keeps compliance as an operational logging practice rather than reshaping R&D purpose. Score 2.\n\nOriginality: degree rare but ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination 2 = interesting 1 = common mundane boring\n\nEmbedded logging architecture with SDK and CI gate is fairly common best practice \"shift left compliance\". Probably common, mundane. So 1