chitecture requires process change, training, spec drafting. Probably some changes, reasonably feasible. Could be 3. Not easy low cost. I'd say 3.\n\napplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nProblem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal directly addresses meeting logging mandate by embedding logs early. So 4.\n\neffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\nWill logging-first solve mandate? Yes ensures compliance, prevents retrofitting. Might be effective. Could be 4. Possibly 3 = contributes to solution. I think it solves main problem of meeting logging mandate. So 4.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nProposal restructures pipeline so logging is from first sprint, not afterthought. It changes relationship? Logging woven into science. That's paradigm stretching? Possibly slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (traceability layer spec) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Does it change relationship between R&D and compliance obligations? It embeds compliance into R&D process. That is changing relationship. Might be 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science). The description: e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or 