em without regard for workability? It maps granularity, gives checklist reducing rework. Probably contributes not fully solve. I'd say 3.\n\nApplicability: degree idea clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related? Do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. Logging mandate compliance. Yes directly related. Score 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem.\n\nOriginality: degree rare but ingenious... Proposal is unusual interesting shows some imagination? Proportional logging taxonomy maybe interesting. Not expressed before AND ingenious? Probably 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Could be 2 interesting. I'd say 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree preserves or modifies paradigm. Options:\n4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving\n\nProposal converts regulatory constraint into publishable contribution strengthening funding applications while giving checklist. That sounds like changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations -> paradigm stretching. Maybe 3. It doesn't break paradigm? It does introduce new elements and change relationship? Converts constraint into publishable contribution = changes relationship. Could be 3. Slightly paradigm stretching would be just tools. This seems more than slightly. I'd pick 3.\n\nNeed output format: echo id, for each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"..._evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer). Likely list.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\nid: P1240\