em without regard for workability. Could be 3 partial? Might be partial because it mentions post-market monitoring but not specific logging items? It says serve as backend required by Article 72 and 12. Likely 4.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities =4. Upgrading infrastructure for compliance is common. So 4.\n\nEvidence: Using existing monitoring for compliance is a common, norm-conforming strategy that does not offend sensibilities.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nEasy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes? It says leverage infrastructure already funded rather than purchasing parallel system. Extends current metrics dashboard with event-log storage, retention scheduling, query interfaces. That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Easy to implement at low cost? Might be moderate cost. Probably 3.\n\nEvidence: The plan reuses funded data-pipeline and dashboard components and adds logging storage and retention, a reasonably feasible extension rather than a new build.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm, anchored to this problem.\nLevels:\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal upgrades existing monitoring to serve compliance. It treats compliance as logging practice using existing tool