ree to which idea can be easily implemented. Easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes? It says small cross-team pod, modular SDK slots into existing MLOps. Might be some changes, reasonably feasible. Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Is it easy at low cost? Maybe not trivial; requires design, integration. Probably level 3 not 4. The description says essentially zero marginal cost after one-time build. That's maybe moderate. I'd say 3.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious... Common, mundane, boring =1. Unusual interesting shows some imagination =3. Not expressed before AND ingenious =4. Reusable middleware for logging is common practice in software compliance. It's common mundane. So originality 1 = common, mundane, boring. Evidence: Shared logging SDKs are a standard MLOps pattern already widely used.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\nLevels:\n4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal is reusable middleware, still treats compliance as logging practice. Introduces new tool but doesn't change relationship fundamentally. That matches level 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Or could be level 1 paradigm preserving? It says shared technical layer... eliminates per-project reimplementation. That's still standard compliance practice. Might be paradigm preserving: standard