        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\n\nWe need rate proposal P2654.\n\nProposal: Author a Logging Specification per System Before Code Freeze. Create mandatory document detailing which events recorded, format, retention, map to risk-management... template tied to Article 12... project leads fill during design review, sign off before build. Keeps technical documentation current...\n\nRate six subscales.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm. Description:\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nProposal is creating a mandatory Logging Specification document before code freeze. That's introducing a new document/process but still treats compliance as logging practice. Not changing relationship fundamentally? It's a process improvement. Likely slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nEvidence sentence: It adds a specification artifact and design-gate review but keeps compliance as separate logging practice rather than redefining R&D.\n\noriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nLogging spec before code freeze is fairly common practice - requirements spec. Might be common/mundane. Could be interesting but common compliance practice. I'd say 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence: Pre-implementation logging specs and design-review sign-offs are standard compliance artifacts in regulated engineering.\n\nacceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat\n1 = radically violates...\n\nCreating mandatory doc is acceptable. 4.\n\nEvidence: Mandating a documentation gate aligns with legal requirements and does not offend norms.\n\nimplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible\n2 = significant change...\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nCreating template and sign-off is easy/low cost. 4? Might be some changes but reasonably feasible. Could be 4 = easy to implement at low cost. Probably 4.\n\nEvidence: A template and design-review checklist can be rolled out with compliance office support without major engineering rework.\n\napplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem...\n2 = may have some benefit...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nProblem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal creates spec to ensure logging meets Article 12. Directly related. So 4.\n\nEvidence: The specification directly addresses the Article 12 mandate to record events and maintain technical documentation for high-risk systems.\n\neffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 = unreasonable...\n1 = solves unrelated...\n\nWill it solve? Spec ensures logging designed in, but doesn't guarantee technical implementation works. It contributes but may not fully solve? Might be partial. The spec maps events to requirements; if followed, should ensure compliance. Could be 4 = will solve stated problem without regard for workability. If you could do it, it would solve main problem of meeting logging mandate. I'd say 3? Hmm.\n\nThe proposal is about authoring spec before code freeze, keeps documentation current, gives auditors artifact. It addresses traceability and compliance. Likely will contribute to solution but not guarantee actual logs work in operation. The effectiveness definition: reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. Spec alone doesn't implement logging, just plans it. Might be partial.\n\nI think 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Because actual logging implementation still needed.\n\nEvidence: A specification improves traceability and audit readiness but does not itself implement the automatic recording capabilities required by Article 12(1).\n\nThus scores:\nparadigm_relatedness 2\noriginality 1\nacceptability 4\nimplementability 4\napplicability 4\neffectiveness 3\n\nProvide output with id and subscale_evidence then subscale.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Just output.\n\nLet's produce clear list.\n\n",
