ctice, acceptable. So 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Evidence: public benchmark is standard research practice and does not contravene regulation.\n\nImplementability (1-4): easy to implement at low cost or non-radical changes? Conduct systematic empirical study with ablation experiments on open-source models. That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Might be significant resources. Could be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Might be 2 = significant change, expensive/difficult but not impossible. For an AI R&D lab committing scarce implementation resources? The proposal is to run experiments and publish leaderboard; that's research activity, maybe feasible. I'd rate 3.\n\nNovelty paradigm_relatedness (1-4): degree idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem. 4=paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3=paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...). 2= slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1=paradigm preserving.\n\nBenchmark of logging overhead is introducing new element (benchmark) but still treats compliance as logging practice? It does change relationship? It's about informing design choices and policymakers. Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new tools/processes but compliance remains logging. I'd say 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree not only rare but ingenious... 4=not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative surprising. 3=unusual interesting shows some imagination. 2=interesting. 1=common mundane boring.\n\nBenchmark of logging overhead in ML systems under Article12 minimum-field requirements. Is that un