ows imaginative integration of legal analysis with ML engineering.\n\nScore 3.\n\nworkability acceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\nProposal fund internal research, publish optionally. Acceptable. Probably 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Research project is acceptable.\n\nEvidence: Funding an internal interdisciplinary research sprint to create compliance guidance is socially, legally and politically unobjectionable.\n\nScore 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\nTwo sprints with small interdisciplinary team prototyping against three deployments. That's reasonably feasible but requires scarce resources. Might be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures.\n\nEvidence: Two sprints with a small cross-functional team is a moderate, reasonably feasible commitment of scarce R&D resources.\n\nScore 3.\n\nReturn rating echo id.\n\nNeed output format: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Instruction says return rating... For each subscale... give ... first and then ... Provide maybe list.\n\nI'll output structured lines.\n\nEnsure order: applicability, effectiveness, paradigm_relatedness, originality, acceptability, implementability\n\nProvide evidence sentence first then score.\n\nLet's produce.",
