cription: slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Checklist and automated simulation tests are new elements? Maybe slight. I'd lean 2.\n\nLet's decide 1? Let's read: \"standard compliance and logging practice; the lab's way of working is unchanged\" That seems fitting. Pre-deployment gate is standard. So paradigm preserving =1.\n\nHmm.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\nProposal ensures logging capabilities verified before deployment, catching gaps pre-deployment avoids costly remediation. That seems solves problem of how to meet logging mandate. Reasonable and will solve? It ensures compliance gate but doesn't guarantee logs operate in real world? Might be partial. Probably 4? It will solve the stated problem without regard for workability: if you could do it, it would solve main problem. Yes.\n\nCould be 3 contributes. I think effectiveness =4.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nIt does X = gate to get Y = logging compliance. Directly related. So 4.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\nGate checklist is acceptable. 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\nChecklist + automated log-simulation tests requires QA team time, compliance office drafting. Some changes. Might be 3? Easy? Probably reasonably feasible, not trivially easy. Could be 3.\n\nIf it's just a gate, might be low cost. Hmm. I think implementability = 3 (some changes, reasonably feasible). Or 4 easy to implement at low cost? Might need engineering effort for simulation tests.\n\nI'll pick 3.\n\nNow output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale in order listed give <subscale>_evidence (one sentence) first then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Might want list.\n\nI'll produce structured output:\n\nid: P1233\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 1\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\noriginality_evidence: The proposal describes a standard pre-deployment compliance gate with checklist and simulation tests that is common in regulated software development. Score 1\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It treats EU logging requirements as conventional logging practice to be verified before release without changing how R&D relates to compliance obligations. Score 1 (or 2). Let's pick 1.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: Verifying logging capabilities against Article 12(2) and 12(3) before deployment would ensure high-risk systems meet the mandated automatic recording and traceability requirements. Score 4\n\napplicability_evidence: The gate directly addresses the lab's need to decide how to meet the Article 12 logging mandate for high-risk AI systems. Score 4\n\nacceptability_evidence: A mandatory compliance checkpoint with standardized verification is a common, legally aligned strategy that violates no norms or sensibilities. Score 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Creating a checklist and running automated log-simulation tests requires some process changes and QA effort but is reasonably feasible for an R&D lab. Score 3\n\nLet's output.\n\n",
